You must not do anything that harms the reputation or integrity of CFA Institute or the designation, or the **integrity, validity or security** of its programs. Programs include more than the CFA exams. Prohibited conduct includes:

- giving or receiving help (cheating) on an exam;
- breaking exam rules or testing policies;
- sharing anything confidential about the program or an exam with other candidates or the public;
- getting around exam security measures;
- using an association with CFA Institute improperly to advance personal or professional goals;
- misrepresenting information to CFA Institute about a program.

All aspects of an exam stay confidential until CFA Institute releases them. That covers specific questions *and* broad topic areas or formulas, whether tested or not tested. You may still discuss **non-confidential** study material with others, including in study groups. The standard applies to you because you are a member or candidate. It does not turn other participants into people bound by the Code unless they are members or candidates.
